\begin_layout Section
Sector accounts
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Our sector accounts match the national accounts,
 except we lump non-profit institutions serving households together with households.
 Each sector also corresponds to one or more agents in the model.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\text{sectors}=\left\{ \text{Households},\text{Government},\text{Financial corporations},\text{Non-financial corporations},\text{Rest of the world}\right\} 
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Sector accounting keeps track of the financial assets and liabilities of all sectors and all transfers between them.
 Non-financial assets are not part of the sector accounts.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A key feature of sector accounting is that all net variables sum to zero over sectors.
 E.g.
 total net debt is zero,
 as any debt liability has a corresponding debt asset.
 Net assets,
 financial income,
 financial transfers between sectors,
 and revaluations all sum to zero.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Sector modules vs sector accounts
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Each sector,
 corresponding to one or more agents,
 has a dedicated module.
 These dedicated modules contain sector-specific specifications of the net financial transactions of the sectors —
 e.g.
 net income and expenditures in the budget constraint.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
These are reconciled with a condensed budget constraint in the sector accounts file,
 which simply states that the period to period change in net assets equals the net financial transactions of the sector plus any revaluation of assets.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Each sector modules must specify how net savings are split between assets and liabilities,
 and between different financial instruments (debt vs equity).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Splitting net positions into assets and liabilities is practical,
 as the alternative is to introduce an additional asset type to distinguish between domestic and foreign equity.
 With explicit liabilities,
 the equity liability of the rest-of-the-world equals the total foreign equity owned by domestic sectors.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Financial stocks and portfolio closure
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For each sector,
 let
\begin_inset Formula $A_{s}^{d}$
\end_inset

 and
\begin_inset Formula $L_{s}^{d}$
\end_inset

 be debt assets and liabilities.
 Let
\begin_inset Formula $A_{s}^{e}$
\end_inset

 and
\begin_inset Formula $L_{s}^{e}$
\end_inset

 be equity assets and liabilities.
 Net financial assets are
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula
\[
N_{s,t}=A_{s,t}^{d}+A_{s,t}^{e}-L_{s,t}^{d}-L_{s,t}^{e}.
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Each financial stock follows the same stock-flow identity:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula
\[
X_{s,t}^{f,a}=\frac{X_{s,t-1}^{f,a}}{fv}+F_{s,t}^{f,a}+R_{s,t}^{f,a}+K_{s,t}^{f,a}.
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Here,
\begin_inset Formula $F$
\end_inset

 is a financial transaction,
\begin_inset Formula $R$
\end_inset

 is a revaluation,
 and
\begin_inset Formula $K$
\end_inset

 is another change in volume.
 A zero transaction keeps the number of claims fixed.
 Revaluation and other volume changes can still change the stock value.
 A sector budget determines the change in net financial assets.
 The portfolio equations below split this change across the four positions.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Households
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Household debt liabilities move part of the way to a fixed share of household consumption:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula
\[
L_{H,t}^{d}=(1-\lambda _{H})\frac{L_{H,t-1}^{d}}{fv}+\lambda _{H}\mu _{H}^{d}C_{t},\qquad 0<\lambda _{H}\leq 1.
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The target ratio
\begin_inset Formula $\mu _{H}^{d}$
\end_inset

 is constant in the forecast.
 The adjustment rate
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda _{H}$
\end_inset

 is 0.2 per year by default.
 Household equity assets have zero transactions.
 Debt assets close the household net financial asset identity:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula
\[
A_{H,t}^{d}=N_{H,t}-A_{H,t}^{e}+L_{H,t}^{d}.
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Households have no equity liability in the model.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Government
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Government debt assets and equity assets have zero transactions.
 Government has no equity liability.
 Debt liabilities close the government net financial asset identity:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula
\[
L_{G,t}^{d}=A_{G,t}^{d}+A_{G,t}^{e}-N_{G,t}.
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A fiscal surplus can make this liability negative.
 The model gives a warning but does not change the value.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Non-financial corporations
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The replacement value of non-financial corporation capital is
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula
\[
V_{N,t}^{K}=\sum_{k}\sum_{i\in N}p_{k,t}^{I}K_{k,i,t}.
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Debt liabilities are a fixed share of this value.
 Debt assets are a fixed share of operating expenses.
 Equity assets are a fixed share of equity liabilities:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula
\[
L_{N,t}^{d}=\mu _{N}^{K}V_{N,t}^{K},\qquad A_{N,t}^{d}=\mu _{N}^{D}E_{N,t},\qquad A_{N,t}^{e}=\mu _{N}^{e}L_{N,t}^{e}.
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Here,
\begin_inset Formula $E_{N,t}$
\end_inset

 is operating expenses.
 Equity liabilities close the sector net financial asset identity:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula
\[
L_{N,t}^{e}=A_{N,t}^{d}+A_{N,t}^{e}-L_{N,t}^{d}-N_{N,t}.
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Financial corporations
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Financial corporations hold a fixed share of total debt liabilities.
 The total includes financial corporation liabilities and thus includes interbank finance:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula
\[
A_{F,t}^{d}=\mu _{F}^{d}\sum_{s}L_{s,t}^{d}.
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Financial corporation equity liabilities have zero transactions.
 Their value changes through revaluation.
 This assumption excludes endogenous equity issues and buy-backs.
 Debt liabilities close the sector net financial asset identity:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula
\[
L_{F,t}^{d}=A_{F,t}^{d}+A_{F,t}^{e}-L_{F,t}^{e}-N_{F,t}.
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Financial corporation equity assets also have zero transactions.
 Their revaluation is a fixed share of the revaluation of non-financial corporation equity liabilities:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula
\[
R_{F,t}^{e,A}=\mu _{F}^{e}R_{N,t}^{e,L}.
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This rule represents a fixed ownership position in non-financial corporations.
 It does not make banks trade equity to keep a fixed value share.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Rest of the world
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Rest-of-world debt liabilities are a fixed share of financial corporation debt assets:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula
\[
L_{R,t}^{d}=\mu _{R}^{d}A_{F,t}^{d}.
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This ratio is the share of bank lending that goes abroad.
 Rest-of-world debt assets close the full debt market:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula
\[
A_{R,t}^{d}=\sum_{s}L_{s,t}^{d}-\sum_{s\ne R}A_{s,t}^{d}.
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
These two debt rules make the debt market clear:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula
\[
\sum_{s}A_{s,t}^{d}=\sum_{s}L_{s,t}^{d}.
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The rest-of-world equity liability is a fixed share of domestic equity assets.
 Rest-of-world equity assets close the equity market:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula
\[
L_{R,t}^{e}=\mu _{R}^{e}\sum_{s\ne R}A_{s,t}^{e},\qquad \sum_{s}A_{s,t}^{e}=\sum_{s}L_{s,t}^{e}.
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Equity values and calibration
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Discounted future equity payouts set financial and non-financial corporation equity values.
 Equity liability revaluation reconciles these values with the stock-flow identity.
 Holder revaluations then allocate the total change across equity assets.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Calibration identifies each fixed portfolio ratio from the calibration-year financial stocks.
 The forecast keeps these ratios constant.
 Financial assets or liabilities can become negative after a large change in a sector balance.
 The model reports a warning and keeps the solved value.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Input output
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The input output system keeps track of all flows of goods and services.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Indices
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
i=\text{industries}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
p=\text{products}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
u=\text{uses}=i\cup\left\{ C,G,X,K,\mathrm{INV}\right\} 
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Where 
\begin_inset Formula $G$
\end_inset

 is government consumption,
 
\begin_inset Formula $C$
\end_inset

 is consumption by households and non-profits,
 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

 is export demand,
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $K$
\end_inset

 is fixed investment,
 while
\begin_inset Formula $\mathrm{INV}$
\end_inset

 is the change in inventories.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Industry split (supply table)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Our default assumption is fixed industry shares for each product.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
Y_{p,i}=\mu_{p,i}Y_{p}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Endogenous industry shares
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
CET split
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
When industry shares are endogenous,
 perfect transformation determines
\begin_inset Formula $\mu_{p,i}$
\end_inset

 and sets
\begin_inset Formula $MC_{p,i}=MC_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 For selected cells,
 CET replaces this closure with
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
Y_{p,i}=\hat{\mu}^{CET}_{p,i}\left(\frac{MC_{p,i}}{MC_{i}}\right)^{E^{CET}_{i}}Y_{i}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $Y_{i}$
\end_inset

 is the CET aggregate.
 CET keeps
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\mu}^{CET}_{p,i}$
\end_inset

 and
\begin_inset Formula $E^{CET}_{i}$
\end_inset

 fixed and determines
\begin_inset Formula $\mu_{p,i}$
\end_inset

 through the output allocation.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $MC_{i}$
\end_inset

 is marginal cost before the product split.
\begin_inset Formula $MC_{p,i}$
\end_inset

 includes product-specific production taxes or subsidies:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
MC_{p,i}=MC^{\text{resource}}_{p,i}+\frac{\partial T^{\text{production}}_{i}}{\partial Y_{p,i}}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
User taxes,
 VAT,
 purchaser-price product taxes,
 and trade margins do not enter
\begin_inset Formula $MC_{p,i}$
\end_inset

;
 they enter the purchaser price.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\sum_{p}MC_{p,i}Y_{p,i}=MC_{i}Y_{i}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Pricing uses
\begin_inset Formula $MC_{p,i}$
\end_inset

;
 CET adds no markup.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Product demand
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Demand modules set purchaser use by product directly.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The input-output system has no use-level demand quantity or product-share parameter.
 A demand module can use fixed shares,
 substitution,
 or other rules to determine
\begin_inset Formula $D_{p,u}$
\end_inset

.
 Inventories are the exception below.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Tourism
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Tourism needs a special entry because the household and trade accounts classify
 the same purchase in different ways.
 The household budget uses the resident concept.
 Thus,
 \begin_inset Formula $C$
\end_inset

 covers purchases by resident households at home and abroad.
 National accounts count purchases in the country by non-resident households
 as exports.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Eurostat reports the household product cells under the domestic concept.
 These cells include purchases in the country by resident and non-resident
 households.
 Eurostat gives only total adjustments for residents' purchases abroad and
 non-residents' purchases in the country.
 We add residents' purchases abroad to the private-consumption cells as travel
 imports.
 We leave non-residents' purchases in these cells and store their total as
 \begin_inset Formula $C^{\mathrm{Tourist}}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The input-output private-consumption aggregate is therefore
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula
\[
D_{C}=C+C^{\mathrm{Tourist}}.
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The model also includes tourist demand in total exports:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula
\[
X=D_{X}+C^{\mathrm{Tourist}},
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
where
 \begin_inset Formula $D_{X}$
\end_inset

 is the direct export column.
 Tourist demand enters the product balance once through
 \begin_inset Formula $D_{C}$
\end_inset

.
 The household budget includes only resident consumption
 \begin_inset Formula $C$
\end_inset

,
 while the rest-of-the-world account includes tourist demand in exports.
 The default uses the combined private-consumption product mix and price for
 resident and tourist demand because Eurostat does not give a separate tourist
 product split.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Country of origin split
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We split all direct product demand by country of origin:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
D_{p,u,o}=\mu_{p,u,o}D_{p,u}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
where
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
o=\left\{ \text{domestic},\text{import}\right\} 
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Using an index for origin makes it easy to split imports later,
 for example into Euro-zone vs rest-of-the-world.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Inventories
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Inventory demand by product and origin,
\begin_inset Formula $D_{p,\mathrm{INV},o}$
\end_inset

,
 is exogenous and can be positive or negative.
 It is zero after the calibration year.
 The fixed product and origin shares above do not apply.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Import-substitution
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To introduce import-substitution with a satellite model,
 endogenize 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu_{p,u,o}$
\end_inset

.
 Substitution by product,
 rather than also by use,
 might be enough.
 Demand responds to relative purchaser prices (see Trade margins):
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
D_{p,u,o}=\hat{\mu}_{p,u,o}\left(\frac{P^{D}_{p,u,o}}{P^{D}_{p,u}}\right)^{-E_{p}}D_{p,u}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Physical energy markets
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Let
\begin_inset Formula $e\in\mathcal{E}\subset p$
\end_inset

 denote an energy product.
 For a homogeneous energy product,
 use the origin choice above.
 Replace
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\mu}_{e,u,o}$
\end_inset

 with
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda_{e,u}\hat{\mu}_{e,u,o}$
\end_inset

 and let physical market clearing determine
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda_{e,u}$
\end_inset

 for each use.
 CET or an energy-technology module allocates domestic output across industries through
\begin_inset Formula $\mu_{e,i}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
National-account and physical units
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The national-account quantities
\begin_inset Formula $D_{e,u,o}$
\end_inset

 and
\begin_inset Formula $Y_{e,i}$
\end_inset

 do not measure physical energy.
 Map them to PJ:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
Q^{D}_{e,u,o}=\kappa^{D}_{e,u,o}D_{e,u,o}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
Q^{D}_{e,u}=\kappa^{D}_{e,u}D_{e,u}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
Q^{Y}_{e,i}=\kappa^{Y}_{e,i}Y_{e,i}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Keep 
\begin_inset Formula $\kappa$
\end_inset

 fixed unless technology changes physical unit content.
 Budgets still use price times national-account quantity.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Total physical demand is
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
Q^{D}_{e}=\sum_{u}Q^{D}_{e,u}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
Quantity-preserving origin demand
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For energy,
 the physical balance for each use is
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\sum_{o}\kappa^{D}_{e,u,o}D_{e,u,o}=\kappa^{D}_{e,u}D_{e,u}=Q^{D}_{e,u}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
It determines
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda_{e,u}$
\end_inset

 in the origin-demand equation above.
 With fixed 
\begin_inset Formula $\kappa$
\end_inset

,
 using prices per PJ only rescales the calibrated CES weights.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
Source balance
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Supply by origin is
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
Q^{S}_{e,o}=\sum_{u}Q^{D}_{e,u,o}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Thus,
 the aggregate physical balance is
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\sum_{o}Q^{S}_{e,o}=Q^{D}_{e}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Domestic supply is
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
Q^{S}_{e,\text{domestic}}=\sum_{i}Q^{Y}_{e,i}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A energy-technology module can endogenize the domestic industry mix,
 but needs to ensure that the supply mix does not break the physical balance.
 A network module can specify losses that need to be added to the balances above.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Use-price differences per PJ come from
\begin_inset Formula $\kappa^{D}_{e,u,o}$
\end_inset

,
 taxes,
 margin services,
 and VAT.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
An energy tax
\begin_inset Formula $\tau^{E}_{e,u,o}$
\end_inset

 per PJ maps to
\begin_inset Formula $t^{E}_{e,u,o}=\tau^{E}_{e,u,o}\kappa^{D}_{e,u,o}$
\end_inset

.
 Emissions use physical fuel at combustion or conversion.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Production (cost minimization)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Given the output to be produced,
 the production module calculates a marginal cost of production and the demand for each input factor into production.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Production function
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We assume a nested CES production function with
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\text{factors of production}=k\cup r\cup l
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
where 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

 are types of capital,
 
\begin_inset Formula $r$
\end_inset

 are types of intermediate inputs,
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $l$
\end_inset

 are types of labor (with one type being perfectly fine).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The default full tree pairs equipment and energy in the first nest
\begin_inset Formula $KE$
\end_inset

,
 then adds labor at
\begin_inset Formula $KEL$
\end_inset

,
 structures at
\begin_inset Formula $KELB$
\end_inset

,
 and materials at the top nest
\begin_inset Formula $KELBM$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Let
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
f=\text{factors of production}\cup\text{nests}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Demand for the factor or nest 
\begin_inset Formula $X_{f}$
\end_inset

 in a specific industry (we omit the 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 index here) is
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
X_{f}=\mu_{f}X_{N}\left(\frac{P_{f}}{P_{N}}\right)^{-E_{N}}\forall f\in N
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
where N is a production nest and 
\begin_inset Formula $P_{f}$
\end_inset

 is the usercost of the production factor or nest.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The usercost of a nest is given by the budget constraint
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
P_{N}X_{N}=\sum_{f\in N}P_{f}X_{f}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Usercost of factors of production can either be simple spot prices,
 or come from complicated usercost expressions.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We implement the nesting structure very compactly,
 using a single variable and demand equation for all factors of production,
 letting a mapping handle the nesting structure.
 The main upside is that this enables us to vary the nesting structure depending on the industry.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Capital adjustment costs and other frictions
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Various frictions should be added to the simple usercost expressions,
 e.g.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
E.g.
 the usercost of capital is something like
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
P^{f}_{k}=P^{I}_{k}+\frac{\partial T^{\text{Corp}}}{\partial K_{k}}-\frac{1-\delta_{k,t+1}}{1+r_{t+1}}\left(P^{I}_{k,t+1}-\frac{\partial T^{\text{Corp}}_{t+1}}{\partial K_{k}}\right)+\text{marginal capital adjustment cost}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The friction itself should be detailed in a satellite model,
 not in the production module.
 Friction variables are hooks that default to zero when no satellite model provides them.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Similarly,
 marginal taxes (here 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{\partial T^{\text{Corp}}}{\partial K_{k}}$
\end_inset

) are defined elsewhere.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Output and marginal cost
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Output is the top nest less friction losses
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
Y=X_{N=\text{TopNest}}-\text{friction losses}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Friction losses can for example be foregone output due to capital adjustment costs.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The marginal cost of production is
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
MC\cdot Y=P_{N=\text{TopNest}}Y+\text{production taxes}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Frictions do not enter this equation directly.
 They affect the price through the usercosts inside the nest:
 the marginal capital adjustment cost enters the usercost of capital,
 and therefore 
\begin_inset Formula $P_{N=\text{TopNest}}$
\end_inset

,
 while the average (total) capital adjustment cost is subtracted from output at the top.
 
\begin_inset Formula $MC$
\end_inset

 is therefore the cost of one more unit of output,
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $MC\cdot Y$
\end_inset

 is not total cost.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Behavior vs accounting
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Usercosts are behavior,
 not accounting.
 The usercost of capital contains the hurdle rate of the firm.
 Marginal cost and the markup derive from this subjective usercost,
 so they are model constructs,
 not neutral accounting.
 The sector account of firms records actual payments instead:
 revenue 
\begin_inset Formula $P^{Y}_{i}Y_{i}$
\end_inset

 less labor compensation,
 intermediate inputs,
 investments,
 and total (not marginal) taxes.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Multiple types of capital
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In the input-output system capital investments enter as a single column.
 In the production module,
 or in a satellite module,
 we split capital investments into multiple types of fixed assets,
 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
D_{K}=\sum_{k}I_{k}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
I_{k}=\sum_{i}I_{k,i}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Fixed investment enters purchaser use by product:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
D_{p,K}=\sum_{k}I_{p,k}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For each type of capital,
 we assume fixed product shares.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
I_{p,k}=\mu_{p,k}I_{k}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $I_{k,i}$
\end_inset

 is given by cost-minimizing a production function subject to capital accumulation laws of motion.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Multiple types of intermediate inputs (energy)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We can split use in production into different types of intermediate inputs
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
D_{p,i}=\sum_{m}\mu_{p,m,i}M_{m,i}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The resulting
\begin_inset Formula $D_{p,i}$
\end_inset

 enters input-output purchaser use directly.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
where for example
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
m=\left\{ \text{energy},\text{materials}\right\}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Until detailed energy data are added,
 synthetic data assign CPA sections B and D to energy and all other products to materials.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
and 
\begin_inset Formula $M_{m,i}$
\end_inset

 is given by cost-minimizing a production function.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Note that assuming identical product shares for energy inputs across industries is not needed and does not simplify much.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Pricing
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Markups in basic prices
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We use one fixed markup for each industry:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
P^{Y}_{p,i}=\theta_{i}\text{MC}_{p,i}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The markup is the same for all products from industry
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

.
 Under the default product split,
\begin_inset Formula $MC_{p,i}=MC_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 The CET module can give product-specific marginal costs.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Basic-price values sum by product and industry:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
P^{Y}_{p}Y_{p}=\sum_{i}P^{Y}_{p,i}Y_{p,i}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
P^{Y}_{i}Y_{i}=\sum_{p}P^{Y}_{p,i}Y_{p,i}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Base-year prices equal one,
 so measured volumes equal values.
 Product-by-use cells therefore do not identify price gaps or markup levels.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Trade and transport margins
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Trade and transport margins are the wholesale,
 retail,
 and transport services needed to deliver a product to its user.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $s=\left\{ \text{wholesale},\text{retail},\text{transport}\right\} \subset p$
\end_inset

 index margin service products.
 Each user has a fixed-share margin-service bundle.
 We use the standard origin shares for each margin service:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
P^{S}_{u}=\sum_{s,o}\mu^{S}_{s,u}\mu_{s,u,o}P_{s,u,o}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $D_{p,u}$
\end_inset

 be purchaser use.
 Each unit needs 
\begin_inset Formula $a^{S}_{p,u}$
\end_inset

 units of the margin service bundle:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
S_{u}=\sum_{p}a^{S}_{p,u}D_{p,u}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
S_{s,u,o}=\mu^{S}_{s,u}\mu_{s,u,o}S_{u}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
S_{p,u,o}=0\quad\forall p\notin s
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Let
\begin_inset Formula $P_{p,u,o}$
\end_inset

 be the domestic basic price or import border price,
 with
\begin_inset Formula $P_{p,u,\text{domestic}}=P^{Y}_{p}$
\end_inset

.
 The purchaser-use price is
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
P^{D}_{p,u,o}=\left(P_{p,u,o}+t_{p,u,o}+a^{S}_{p,u}P^{S}_{u}\right)\left(1+t^{\text{VAT}}_{p,u,o}\right)
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This is the price that enters into any product substitution decision.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The product balance is
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
Y_{p,u}=D_{p,u,\text{domestic}}+S_{p,u,\text{domestic}}.
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Margin demand enters the product balance but not the direct-demand quantity sum.
 Its value is already in the purchaser price of direct demand,
 so do not add
\begin_inset Formula $P^{S}_{u}S_{u}$
\end_inset

 to the user budget.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Households
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The household module determines total demand for private consumption (consumption savings decision).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Consumption savings decision
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Our default has one forward-looking household.
 It chooses total consumption
\begin_inset Formula $C_{t}$
\end_inset

 and end-of-period real wealth
\begin_inset Formula $W_{t}$
\end_inset

.
 A fixed share of current real disposable income follows a hand-to-mouth rule:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
C^{\mathrm{HtM}}_{t}=\lambda_{\mathrm{HtM}}Y^{d}_{t},\qquad0\leq\lambda_{\mathrm{HtM}}\leq1.
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Thus,
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda_{\mathrm{HtM}}$
\end_inset

 is a share of income,
 not a share of households.
 The household takes the external habit
\begin_inset Formula $H_{t}=h\bar{C}_{t-1}$
\end_inset

 as given.
 Define consumption in excess of the reference level as
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
C^{\mathrm{xRef}}_{t}=C_{t}-H_{t}-C^{\mathrm{HtM}}_{t}.
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The household solves
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\max_{\{C_{s},W_{s}\}^{\infty}_{s=t}}\sum^{\infty}_{s=t}\beta^{s-t}\left[\frac{(C^{\mathrm{xRef}}_{s})^{1-\sigma}}{1-\sigma}+\omega\frac{W^{1-\nu}_{s}}{1-\nu}\right].
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Use the simple budget constraint
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
W_{t}=(1+r_{t})W_{t-1}+Y^{d}_{t}-C_{t},
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
where
\begin_inset Formula $r_{t}$
\end_inset

 is the real interest rate.
 The first-order condition for saving gives the Euler equation
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
(C^{\mathrm{xRef}}_{t})^{-\sigma}=\omega W^{-\nu}_{t}+\beta(1+r_{t+1})(C^{\mathrm{xRef}}_{t+1})^{-\sigma}.
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Private consumption types
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We split private consumption into types 
\begin_inset Formula $c\in\mathcal{C}$
\end_inset

.
 The same product can enter more than one type,
 such as electricity for heating and transport.
 For each type,
 the default has fixed product shares:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
D_{p,C}=\sum_{c\in\mathcal{C}}\mu_{p,c}C_{c}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The resulting
\begin_inset Formula $D_{p,C}$
\end_inset

 enters input-output purchaser use directly.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A type-specific module can replace the fixed product shares with technology choices.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Consumption types are the leaves of a CES tree.
 Let
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
a=\mathcal{C}\cup\text{consumption nests}.
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
C_{a}=\mu_{a}C_{N}\left(\frac{P_{a}}{P_{N}}\right)^{-E_{N}}\quad\forall a\in N,
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
P_{N}C_{N}=\sum_{a\in N}P_{a}C_{a}.
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
At the root,
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
C_{N=\mathrm{TopNest}}=C.
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Exports
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We model export demand directly for domestic production of each product,
 
\begin_inset Formula $Y_{p,X}$
\end_inset

,
 using the final purchaser price 
\begin_inset Formula $P^{D}_{p,X,\text{domestic}}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Imports for re-export,
 
\begin_inset Formula $D_{p,X,\text{import}}$
\end_inset

,
 are completely exogenous.
 To accommodate this,
 the import share of exports,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu_{p,X,o}$
\end_inset

,
 is endogenized.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Government
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Sector accounting.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Including basic demand for public consumption and government transfers to other sectors.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
There is no fiscal closure by default:
 government debt drifts.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Taxes
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Tax accounting and marginal tax rates (derivatives of total revenues wrt.
 to various tax bases).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Ideally,
 other modules should only use aggregate marginal tax rates and aggregate tax revenues.
 E.g.,
 a household budget contains a total taxes paid variable,
 while a labor market decision equation uses only a marginal tax on labor income.
 The break down of marginal taxes into income brackets etc.
 is kept in the taxes module.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Labor market
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The default model is a forward-looking Phillips curve with exogenous labor supply.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Firms
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Sector accounting and endogenous revaluation rate of equity through a discounted cash flow valuation of the firm.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
One module covers both financial and non-financial corporations.
 The non-financial sector sums profits over industries;
 the sector accounting system redistributes them to owners.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Rest of the world
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Closure of the sector accounting system and financial markets.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Foreign prices and fixed interest rate from ECB belongs here.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Aggregates
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Accounting aggregates not covered by other parts of the model.
 E.g.
 GDP.
\end_layout

\end_body
\end_document
